<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Orleans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Orleans is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> near its mouth, founded in 1718 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-le-moyne-de-bienville">Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville</a>. It became the capital of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1763 and returned briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> culture and river trade made it the commercial centre of the Gulf coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-orleans.jpg" alt="New Orleans" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Bannister · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Orleans_from_the_Air_September_2019_-_Central_Business_District_Skyline_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1862 <b>New Orleans</b> fell on 29 April, and the two invasions of Kentucky and Maryland ended at Antietam, which Allan Nevins calls the strategic high-water mark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Posts and missions such as Detroit, founded in 1701, Mobile and <b>New Orleans</b>, founded in 1718, became lasting cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1791 and 1810 more than 25,000 Creoles, planters, petits blancs and free people of colour with their slaves, fled to the United States, Jamaica and Cuba; more than 10,000, expelled from Cuba, reached <b>New Orleans</b> in 1809–1810, nearly doubling its population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 20th century Haitian migrants took it to eastern Cuba, and later migrants to the United States, where it drew African Americans and Caribbean migrants, mixed with Santería and Spiritism, and fed the <b>New Orleans</b> Voodoo Revival.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>New Orleans</b> in 1860 it was the hit of the Rumsey and Newcomb Minstrels, and Mrs. John Wood's singing in a burlesque drew seven encores; the publisher P. P. Werlein printed a version without the dialect.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dixie">Dixie</a></li><li><blockquote>On 4 January 1961 the Dutch jazz group <b>New Orleans</b> Syncopators recorded an arrangement by their leader Jan Burgers as &quot;Midnight in Moscow&quot;; Kenny Ball and his Jazzmen used the same arrangement in November 1961, and their record reached number two on the UK Singles Chart in January…</blockquote><a href="/songs/moscow-nights">Moscow Nights</a></li></ul></section>
